{
  "dom_id": "step-t1648",
  "name": "Introduction",
  "problem": "Up to now, all the parabolas we've seen have been written in **standard form**.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/differential/14244048/assets/e3fb2f9598362bc373e475faf19bb84623a73281da549bf85018f22b7f8a095d.svg)\n\nWe can also write the equation of a parabola in the so-called **vertex form,** given by\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/differential/14244048/assets/4dc70525f24857744b03c32f2f558b27691c823f17bab16a569d3bbdadab732b.svg)\n\nWhen a parabola is written in vertex form, the pair ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/differential/14244048/assets/3a6333dccc007be0992f95974a58ce3d652e5ecafcaaa865a24ff8b42bcfc37a.svg) gives the coordinates of its vertex.\n\nFor example, consider the following parabola: \n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/differential/14244048/assets/2366bd4f3ea2bcb500a85b13c29a3b512d0529ffc82e507c81dae3df96176981.svg)\n\nThis parabola is written in vertex form with ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/differential/14244048/assets/b4edfee225234d67bf8c5a1f8283fe3eb5edba258f6268e9ffe7e604def55858.svg) and ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/differential/14244048/assets/6fa7648f1eeb4effdf9b60cf5199b0ac26e1c1d57c0f05d9e9bd895e5c277c21.svg) Therefore, the vertex of the parabola is at ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/differential/14244048/assets/2bea2c53403b70e552b749cbbf6a3eba65ebdf56f2028ee7907a6700bbedafc4.svg)\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/differential/14244048/assets/4e050c9f52965d9682cae055c1264ac2db9a58b09557f6e15f0178e9375b3872.png)\n\nIn future lessons, we'll learn how to express any parabola in vertex form. For now, let's get some practice working with this form.",
